**Read:** [Fatal liquid nitrogen release — CSB Foundation Food Group investigation](../sources/Foundation-Food-Group-Investigation-CSB.lingua#r_7KXAYPC80QQ764WA8DKSJTDRGA), single-point failure and monitoring sections; [Reliability-centered maintenance — NASA, September 2008](../sources/Reliability-Centered-Maintenance-NASA.lingua#r_08ZD3ATSYCJX5586EYSY0M4N3R), failure analysis; [Guideline for Safe Practices for Cryogenic Air Separation Plants — EIGA 147/22](../sources/Cryogenic-Air-Separation-EIGA.lingua#r_39BST0TM9XV9VEZ14NXVJW7TWA), control and training passages.

A control loop regulates production. An alarm communicates a condition. A protective action changes the process when specified conditions occur. They may share a sensor, power source, communication path or final valve. Giving them different names does not make their failures independent.

Describe each function from process condition through sensing, decision, action and resulting physical state. Include loss of signal and loss of power. A command acknowledgement may establish software behavior without proving valve travel, isolation or acceptable process conditions.

**Work:** Trace a hypothetical high-level protection and a low-pressure service alarm. Identify common dependencies and define an observable end-to-end test for each. Distinguish tests of the logic, the final element and the full physical function. Assign no safety-integrity rating from this qualitative exercise.

